# XCU test
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# test and [ may be built into the shell, but XCU 1.6 also requires them
# to be reachable by exec. Every check therefore runs four ways: test and
# [ , each called by the shell and by a direct exec of the name (argv[0]
# is the name given, as the page asks). Standard input, output and error
# are redirected for the calls, so none is a terminal. Tests of primaries
# that depend on file permissions use only cases that hold for a
# privileged and an unprivileged user alike.

assertion  XCU.test.001
anchor     utilities/test.html#OPERANDS
anchor     utilities/test.html#EXIT STATUS
class      required
since      1992
says       With no argument (or only the closing bracket of [) the result
           is false, exit status one.
test       t/001-zero-args.sh

assertion  XCU.test.002
anchor     utilities/test.html#OPERANDS
class      required
since      1992
says       With one argument the result is true (zero) when it is not the
           null string, even when it looks like an operator (-n, !, =,
           -f), and false when it is empty.
test       t/002-one-arg.sh

assertion  XCU.test.003
anchor     utilities/test.html#OPERANDS
class      required
since      1992
says       -n string is true for a non-empty string and false for an empty
           one; -z string is the opposite; a string that looks like an
           operator is still just a string.
test       t/003-n-z.sh

assertion  XCU.test.004
anchor     utilities/test.html#OPERANDS
class      required
since      1992
says       s1 = s2 is true when the strings are identical and s1 != s2
           when they are not, including when one or both are empty.
test       t/004-string-equal.sh

assertion  XCU.test.005
anchor     utilities/test.html#OPERANDS
class      required
since      1992
says       In the POSIX locale, s1 < s2 and s1 > s2 compare strings by
           their byte order: a before b, A before a, and a string before
           a longer string it starts.
test       t/005-string-order.sh

assertion  XCU.test.006
anchor     utilities/test.html#OPERANDS
class      required
since      1992
says       -eq, -ne, -gt, -ge, -lt and -le compare integers algebraically,
           including negative numbers and numbers that differ in digit
           count (10 is greater than 9).
test       t/006-integers.sh

assertion  XCU.test.007
anchor     utilities/test.html#OPERANDS
class      required
since      1992
says       -e is true for an existing file of any kind, -f for a regular
           file, -d for a directory, -L and -h for a symbolic link
           (including one whose target is missing) and -p for a FIFO; each
           is false for a path that does not exist or is another kind.
test       t/007-file-types.sh

assertion  XCU.test.008
anchor     utilities/test.html#OPERANDS
class      required
since      1992
says       -s is true for a file with data and false for an empty one;
           -r, -w and -x are true for a file the user can read, write,
           and run (or search, for a directory).
test       t/008-size-access.sh

assertion  XCU.test.009
anchor     utilities/test.html#OPERANDS
class      required
since      1992
says       f1 -nt f2 and f1 -ot f2 compare last modification times, with a
           file that exists counting as newer than one that cannot be
           resolved; -ef is true for two names of the same file.
test       t/009-times-same-file.sh

assertion  XCU.test.010
anchor     utilities/test.html#OPERANDS
class      required
since      1992
says       A symbolic link is followed by every primary except -h and -L: a
           link to a regular file is -f and -s, a link to a directory is
           -d, a link to nothing is not -e.
test       t/010-follow-links.sh

assertion  XCU.test.011
anchor     utilities/test.html#OPERANDS
class      required
since      1992
says       The ! operator negates: with two arguments, ! "" is true and
           ! x is false, and with a primary and with three or four
           arguments the result of the rest is negated; the lone argument
           ! is the string !.
test       t/011-negation.sh

assertion  XCU.test.012
anchor     utilities/test.html#OPERANDS
class      required
since      1992
says       With three arguments a binary primary in the middle takes
           precedence over ! as the first argument, so ! = ! is the
           comparison of two equal strings and ! = x is a comparison of
           different ones.
test       t/012-three-args-binary.sh

assertion  XCU.test.013
anchor     utilities/test.html#OPERANDS
class      required
since      1992
says       -t fd is false for a descriptor that is not open and for one open
           on a regular file or the null device.
test       t/013-t-false.sh

assertion  XCU.test.014
anchor     utilities/test.html#OPTIONS
class      required
since      1992
says       The argument -- is not treated as the end of options: test --
           is true (one non-empty argument) and test -z -- is false.
test       t/014-double-dash.sh
